{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nA single Gerrit Verified run needs 21 concurrent job slots. The navapbc org is on GitHub Free: 20 total concurrent jobs and 5 concurrent macOS, enforced PER ORGANIZATION and shared with every other navapbc repo. So one run, in an otherwise idle org, cannot fully parallelize — and under a 6-change burst later runs queued 6.7-8.4m for a macOS slot, which is what turns a ~21m run into the 41-47m seen from Gerrit.\n\n`.github/workflows/_optionality.yml` contributes 8 of those 21 jobs. Each does 0.4-0.8m of real work but pays a full checkout + setup-python + pip install and consumes a slot for the duration.\n\n## Approach\nTwo independent reductions, neither of which weakens the optionality contract.\n\n1. DELETE the `import-linter` job. It installs `-e .[dev]` — the SAME environment as the `test` matrix cells — and runs `pytest tests/unit/test_core_optionality.py tests/unit/test_optional.py`. Verified: those two files carry NO pytest markers and their 9 tests collect under `-m \"not integration and not external\"`, so all four `test` matrix cells in `_build-and-test.yml` already run them in the identical environment. `build-and-test` is in `vote.needs`, so the gating survives.\n\n2. COLLAPSE the 5 `per-extra` cells + the `union` cell into ONE job whose bash loop has SIX iterations, each in its own freshly-created venv:\n   - five ISOLATION iterations, one per extra (agents, eval, tracing, mcp, grounding), each installing ONLY `.[<extra>]`; plus\n   - one UNION iteration installing `.[agents,eval,tracing]` together into a SINGLE venv.\n   The union iteration is NOT redundant with the five: it is the only thing that exercises JOINT dependency resolution and can surface a `ResolutionImpossible` that no single-extra install can. Dropping it, or \"covering\" it with five separate venvs, would be a silent coverage loss.\n\nEach isolation iteration must reproduce ALL of the work the current matrix cell does, not just the install:\n   - `import rebar` with the extra present (installing an extra must not break the core import); and\n   - for extras in `_optional.EXTRAS`, assert `_optional.extra_installed(extra)` — the detection probe; `mcp` has no probe and is validated by the boot check below.\n   - `rebar-mcp --help` for the mcp iteration ONLY, reproducing the current `if: matrix.extra == 'mcp'` conditional. Running it for every extra would fail in venvs that lack the entry point; dropping it would silently weaken the mcp boot check.\n\nError isolation is explicit, because GitHub Actions runs `run:` steps under `bash -e`: a naive loop aborts on the first failing extra and leaves the rest untested. The loop uses an exit-code accumulator — each iteration's install+checks are guarded with `|| { failed=1; echo \"::error::extra <x> FAILED\"; continue; }` and the step ends with `exit ${failed:-0}` — reproducing the `fail-fast: false` semantics the matrix provided today.\n\nKEEP `clean-core-wheel` as its own job, unmodified. It is the check this file's own header credits with catching \"the module-scope-import class that escaped once\", it builds a real wheel rather than an editable install, and it runs a pytest selection inside the lean venv. Leaving the highest-value check structurally untouched is deliberate.\n\nRejected alternative: sequential `pip install .[agents]` then `.[eval]` into one shared python. This is the footgun that must never ship — after the first install the environment is contaminated, so a missing dependency declaration in a later extra would PASS silently. That is precisely the bug class this workflow exists to catch.\nRejected alternative: one venv per extra with NO union iteration — loses joint-resolution coverage (see above).\nRejected alternative: collapsing `clean-core-wheel` in too — more slot savings, but it concentrates the one check with a proven escape history into shared machinery.\n\n## Scope\n- `.github/workflows/_optionality.yml` — remove the `import-linter` job; replace the `per-extra` matrix job and the `union` job with one job running the six-iteration venv loop described above.\n\nExplicitly NOT in scope: `clean-core-wheel` (unchanged), `_build-and-test.yml`, and `gerrit-verify.yaml` (the caller references the reusable by name, not by inner job).\n\n## Testing\nNo new testable behavior in the library — this is CI workflow configuration, so no unit test is warranted. The contract is preserved by construction (fresh venv per iteration) and verified by RUNNING it: `optionality.yml` triggers on `pull_request`, and for PR events GitHub resolves a `./` reusable from the PR merge ref rather than the default branch, so a throwaway PR against the GitHub mirror exercises the modified reusable end-to-end pre-merge. That is the validation path, and it is available here even though the Gerrit `workflow_dispatch` lane cannot exercise workflow-file changes.\n\nFail-isolation must be demonstrated, not assumed: temporarily add a deliberately broken extra name to the loop and confirm the run reports the failure, CONTINUES through the remaining iterations, and exits non-zero. Remove it before landing.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each of the five extras is installed into its own freshly-created venv, never into a venv or python that already had another extra installed.\n- [ ] A sixth UNION iteration installs `.[agents,eval,tracing]` together into one venv, preserving the joint-resolution / `ResolutionImpossible` check.\n- [ ] Each isolation iteration runs `import rebar` and, for extras in `_optional.EXTRAS`, asserts `_optional.extra_installed(extra)`.\n- [ ] `rebar-mcp --help` runs for the mcp iteration only.\n- [ ] With a deliberately broken iteration injected, the remaining iterations still run and the job exits non-zero; the broken entry is removed before landing.\n- [ ] The `clean-core-wheel` job is byte-identical to its current form.\n- [ ] `tests/unit/test_core_optionality.py` and `tests/unit/test_optional.py` still run in the default suite (they are the coverage the deleted `import-linter` job provided).\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_verify_gate_parity.py` passes.\n- [ ] `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py` passes.\n- [ ] `make lint` passes (actionlint covers all workflows)."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785445593414238001,"uuid":"5122e695-81db-4b20-bb97-c24154e5b168"}